BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:2026-10-14_1758_facebook_first-10-minutes-with-ai.md@social-squad-sched
 ule
DTSTAMP:20261002T095800Z
DTSTART:20261002T095800Z
DTEND:20261002T102800Z
SUMMARY:Facebook: Your first 10 minutes with AI (free\, nothing to install)
DESCRIPTION:approved - You've heard that everyone is using AI. You've maybe
  made an account. And now you're looking at an empty box\, wondering what 
 a "good" question even looks like\, and whether you're about to do somethi
 ng dumb.\n\nYou're not. There's nothing to break here\, and it costs nothi
 ng. Only 34% of workers say their employer offers any AI training\, so mos
 t people are teaching themselves. This is the shortest version of that I k
 now.\n\n**Step 1: Pick one real task.**\nSomething from your actual week t
 hat you mildly dislike. A tricky email\, a meeting summary\, a formula you
  can never remember. Don't pick "learn AI." Pick a task.\n\n**Step 2: Open
  a free tool.**\nClaude\, ChatGPT and Gemini all have free plans. Make an 
 account with your email. Cost is the barrier 57% of people name\, so start
  on the free plan and stay there until you have a reason to leave it.\n\n*
 *Step 3: Strip out anything confidential.**\nThis is the step people skip\
 , so it comes before your first prompt. A free tool is a company's server\
 , and what you type goes there. Depending on the tool and the plan\, chats
  may be reviewed or used to improve the model. Defaults differ\, so open t
 he privacy or data settings in your account and read what yours does.\n\nT
 hen build one habit. Before you paste anything\, swap client and colleague
  names for placeholders ("Client A")\, round the numbers\, and delete what
 ever identifies a deal\, a person or a price. Keep the shape of the proble
 m and lose the details that point to a real one.\n\nSome things never go i
 n at all: passwords\, contracts\, anything your company has told you to ke
 ep in-house. If your employer has an AI policy\, follow it. If you're not 
 sure\, ask\, or use a made-up example. Worry about privacy is one of the t
 op reasons people hold back from AI\, and it's a fair worry. This step is 
 how you answer it.\n\n**Step 4: Give it a situation\, then a request.**\nA
 n empty prompt gets you a generic answer. Try this one\, with your own det
 ails changed:\n\n*"I work in finance. Here's a draft email to my manager a
 bout a budget overrun on Project X: [paste\, with names and exact figures 
 changed]. Make it shorter and calmer\, and keep the numbers."*\n\nYour sit
 uation first\, your request second. That's the whole formula.\n\n**Step 5:
  Push back.**\nSay "too formal." Say "put the number first." Say "give me 
 a version for someone who's annoyed." The first answer is a draft\, and th
 e skill you're building is steering it.\n\n**Step 6: Check it.**\nAI write
 s confident sentences\, including wrong ones. Read every number and every 
 claim against your source before you send anything. Then put your real nam
 es and figures back in yourself. This takes two minutes and it keeps you o
 ut of trouble.\n\n## Free videos to watch next\n\nWatching helps\, especia
 lly if you follow along with your own task open in another window. Search 
 these channels:\n\n- **Anthropic (official):** walkthroughs of Claude from
  the people who make it.\n- **OpenAI (official):** the same for ChatGPT.\n
 - **Google (official):** intro videos on Gemini.\n\nCheck the dates on wha
 tever you click. These tools change quickly\, and a video from a year ago 
 may show a different screen.\n\n## What's next\n\nYou've now used AI on re
 al work\, safely. The problem is that it lives in a chat window and disapp
 ears. In Part 2\, I'll set up VS Code and GitHub step by step so you can k
 eep your work\, save every version\, and let AI help inside your own files
 . Under an hour\, and every step stands on its own.\n\nCheers!
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
